<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.13.5: Graphing Quadratic Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01977</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.13.5: Graphing Quadratic Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>2515f08c-2e11-439a-90a0-b426e4837379</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>Consider the quadratic equation \(y=x^2-4x\). Consider the graph of the equation when answering questions a and b.</item>
      </list>
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Where are the \(x\)-intercepts located?</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>The \(x\)-intercepts are at \(x=0\) and \(x=4\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <title>Cool Down Activity</title>
        <exercise id="_167a80b4-40da-4f97-8af6-067a60705252" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The vertex is halfway between the intercepts at \(x=2\).</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 2. The vertex is halfway between the intercepts at \(x=2\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Here is a graph of \(y=x^2\).</para>
            <para>
                      <media alt="&quot;Graph of Parabola y = x squared&amp;nbsp;&quot; height=&quot;200&quot;">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_15_12_01.svg"/>
                </media>
              
           </para>
            <para>Sketch this graph, then sketch a graph of \(y=x^2+5x\) on the same graph. Briefly explain how you know where to sketch the graph.</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>\(x^2+5x\) is equivalent to \(x(x+5)\), so the \(x\)-intercepts are at \(x=0\) and \(x=-5\). Calculate the vertex by finding the \(x\)-coordinate in between \(x = -5\) and \(x = 0\) which is \(x = -2.5\). Substitute \(x=-2.5\) into the equation to find the \(y\)-coordinate: \(y=(-2.5)2+5(-2.5)=-6.188\). The vertex is at \((-2.5, -6.188)\). The squared term has a positive coefficient so the graph opens upward. Adding a positive linear term to \(x^2\) shifts the graph down and to the left.</para>
            <para>
                      <media alt="&quot;Graph of 2 parabolas &amp;nbsp;&quot; height=&quot;200&quot;">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_15_12_02.svg"/>
                </media>
              
           </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
    </section>
  </content>
